---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro'
import { assertion, tableFor } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const stable = assertion('eighty-eight-per-cent-never-changed')
const once = assertion('most-of-what-changed-changed-once')
const field = assertion('the-largest-change-is-a-field-changing-its-mind')
const family = assertion('urban-and-agricultural-change-are-close')
const disagree = assertion('two-files-disagree-about-how-much-moves')

const rate = assertion('the-rate-of-change-falls-five-to-one')
const peak = assertion('the-peak-moves-when-you-divide')
const bias = assertion('a-first-date-misses-a-tenth-of-the-changes')
const forest = assertion('the-forest-in-equilibrium-was-cut')

const canopy = assertion('canopy-is-where-it-was-and-not-where-it-was')
const bothWays = assertion('more-ground-lost-canopy-than-gained-it')
const edge = assertion('the-thin-edge-of-the-canopy-is-going')
const consolidating = assertion('the-canopy-is-consolidating')
const urbanised = assertion('canopy-falls-where-the-land-urbanised')

const epochs = tableFor('measure/allen-county-land-cover-change-dates-2001-2021.yml')

const fieldSpec = bar(
  field.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  field.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Acres of Allen County, 1985 to 2023', labelValues: true },
)
const rateSpec = bar(
  rate.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  rate.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Acres first changing class, per year', labelValues: true },
)
const edgeSpec = bar(
  edge.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  edge.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Acres of ground', labelValues: true },
)
const urbanisedSpec = bar(
  urbanised.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  urbanised.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Percentage points of canopy moved, 2011 to 2021', labelValues: true },
)
---

<Article slug="a-field-that-changed-its-mind">
  <p>
    Satellites have classified every thirty-metre square of Allen County once a year since 1985.
    Nine of those years were already read here, as separate photographs taken three years apart. The
    thirty-nine-year run behind them answers a different question: not what the ground was on nine
    occasions, but how often it moved at all, and when.
  </p>

  <h2>Eleven acres in a hundred</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={stable} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={once} />
  <p>
    That is the shape of a county that is not doing very much: nine acres in ten never reclassified
    once in thirty-nine years, and three quarters of what did move moved a single time. Then there
    is a long thin tail — a hundred and twelve acres that changed ten times or more, and one square
    of ground, two tenths of an acre, that changed twenty-five times.
  </p>

  <h2>What changed, and it is not what you would guess</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={field} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="kinds"
    title="The two largest kinds of land cover change"
    caption="The change index resolves each square to one dominant category across the thirty-nine years. Agriculture changing inside its own class is a field alternating between row crop and hay."
    columns={['Kind of change', 'Acres']}
    rows={field.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString()])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="kinds" set:html={JSON.stringify(fieldSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    A field sown to corn in one year and left in hay the next has changed land cover class. Nothing
    was built and nobody moved; the classifier is doing exactly its job and reporting what is
    growing. In a county that is seven tenths farmed, that single fact is the largest thing in the
    change record, and it is invisible in a file that only photographs the ground every three years
    — because a rotation that comes back around lands in the same class both times.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={family} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={disagree} open />

  <h2>When</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={rate} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="rate"
    title="How fast the county was changing"
    caption="Acres first changing land cover class per year, at the busiest step of the 2000s and at the last step of the record."
    columns={['Period', 'Acres a year']}
    rows={rate.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(1)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="rate" set:html={JSON.stringify(rateSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={peak} open />
  {
    epochs && (
      <DataTable
        title="Eight steps, two and three years long"
        caption="The two right-hand columns are the same eight rows ranked twice. Five of them move."
        columns={epochs.columns}
        rows={epochs.rows}
      />
    )
  }
  <p>
    The steps are not the same length, and a column of acres against a column of date ranges looks
    like a series when it is a series of eight differently sized things. Divided by their own
    durations the busiest years in Allen County are 2004 to 2006, not 2008 to 2011, and the step
    that looked fifth is second. The collapse is in both readings — five to one either way — but a
    reference that named the wrong decade would have told a reader something specific and false
    about their own.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={bias} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={forest} open />

  <h2>The trees, which are the same and are not</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={canopy} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={bothWays} />
  <p>
    Two hundred acres net across eleven years is nothing at all, and it is sitting on top of
    twenty-four thousand acres of ground where the canopy moved one way or the other. The total is
    the least interesting number in the file.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={edge} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="edge"
    title="Ground by how much canopy it carries"
    caption="Acres of Allen County at or above a tenth canopy cover, and at or above a half, at each end of the run. The first falls in every year of the eleven; the second rises in almost all of them."
    columns={['Measure', 'Acres']}
    rows={edge.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString()])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="edge" set:html={JSON.stringify(edgeSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={consolidating} />
  <p>
    What is going is the fringe: the fencerow with four trees in it, the scrubby corner, the strip
    along a ditch. What is thickening is the woodlot. A county can hold the same quantity of tree
    and have far fewer places with a tree in them, and this one is doing that at about four hundred
    acres a year.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={urbanised} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="where"
    title="Where the canopy went"
    caption="Mean change in per-cent canopy cover between 2011 and 2021, on the squares each change category claims. Two of the three are falls."
    columns={['Where the land cover changed', 'Points of canopy']}
    rows={urbanised.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(3)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="where" set:html={JSON.stringify(urbanisedSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Building costs trees and farming does not. That is not a surprise and it is worth having as a
    figure rather than as an expectation, because the two are measured by different instruments on
    the same grid and they agree. What neither instrument can say is which tree, or whose.
  </p>
</Article>
